# Lornes Credit Bureau (LCB) v1.0 — Reference Deployment

Purpose
- LCB converts the Toll Commitment doctrine into a measurable enforcement system.
- Provides a ledger, reservation tracking, feedback ingestion (observations), and toll-levying endpoints to enable a feedback loop and compliance monitoring.

Design goals (v1.0)
- Lightweight, auditable ledger backed by Postgres
- HTTP API for reservations, feedback events, and enforcement actions
- Fast iteration: docker-compose for dev; Kubernetes/Helm recommended for production
- Audit log of all actions for enforcement and dispute resolution

Contents
- app/ — FastAPI service
- migrations/init.sql — DDL for Postgres tables
- docker-compose.yml — Postgres + LCB service for local testing
- Dockerfile — builds the LCB app image
- .env.example — env vars for config

Quick start (local)
1. Copy files into a directory.
2. Create .env from .env.example and optionally adjust.
3. docker-compose up --build
4. Service available at http://localhost:8000
5. OpenAPI docs: http://localhost:8000/docs

Core API (high level)
- POST /reservations — create a reservation (resource usage placeholder)
- GET /reservations/{id} — view reservation
- POST /feedback — ingest observation/feedback (e.g., evidence of siphon behavior)
- POST /enforce/levy — levy toll against an account based on evidence
- GET /accounts/{account_id} — get ledger balances and history
- GET /health — basic health check

Operational checklist after deployment
- Configure TLS and authentication (mTLS / OAuth2) for production
- Configure retention and auditing (WAL offsite, immutable logs)
- Integrate with detection systems that send /feedback events
- Configure alerting (Prometheus + Alertmanager + Slack PagerDuty)
- Add dispute-resolution workflow & human review queue

Next steps
- Add RBAC and signed events for non-repudiation
- Add rate-limits and quotas
- Implement deterministic toll calculations and policy store
- Provide a UI/dashboard for investigations

